  "query_preview": "..Radar was showing scattered showers and isolated\nthunderstorms moving west-northwest across the area this morning\nlikely associated with a disturbance aloft. South of the border\na decaying MCS likely sending outflows to the west-northwest\nwhich will develop isolated storms near/along the Intl border\nthis morning. Some of the showers this morning have had some \npretty good rainfall rates, up to 1\"/hr. The CAMs this morning \nare absolutely lost with what is currently going on. Argh.\n\nOnce these showers/storms end later this morning, we are looking\nat another active mid-afternoon into this evening with heavy \nrain and localized severe wind gusts the main concerns. Flash \nflood watch in effect from 2 pm to 11 pm from Tucson east.\nPotential again exists for activity to continue into the\novernight periods Wednesday morning. \n\nWednesday: Flow aloft starts to turn out of south with upper \nhigh moving into central TX and an upper level trof moving into \nthe west coast. Still plenty of moisture around for continued \nchance of afternoon and evening thunderstorms will the usual \nthreats associated with them.\n\nThursday: The upper level trof to our west turns the upper level\nflow to the southwest and start to import some drier air aloft.\nPlenty of moisture still around for another round of afternoon \nand evening thunderstorms with storm motion now to the northeast\ninstead of the west-northwest during the past week or so. Aft \n11/06z, ISOLD -SHRA mainly W of KTUS diminishing. Lower cloud \ndecks also diminishing. Friday into the weekend: Significant \ndownturn in daily thunderstorm activity as drier air pushes \nacross the area under southwest flow aloft. Isolated \nthunderstorms at best, and mostly east of Tucson during these 3 \ndays. \n\nMonday: Flow aloft returns to the east-southeast resulting in a\nslight uptick in storms. Nothing special.\n\nTemperatures will be near normal today, then be about 3 to 6 \ndegrees below normal Wednesday and Thursday, with warming \nthereafter to above normal values for this weekend.",
